'use strict';
/* ───────────── Reading and Writing question bank ─────────────
   Every passage and question here was written for this app in the style of the Digital SAT.
   Fields: skill, d (1 easy · 2 medium · 3 hard), p passage (HTML), q question, c choices, a index of the answer, x explanation.
   Choices are shuffled when shown, so explanations name choices by their words, never by letter. */

const RW_BANK = [
  /* ── Central Ideas and Details ── */
  { skill: 'cid', d: 1, p: 'Honeybee colonies keep their hives at about 35°C even in cold weather. Worker bees cluster tightly around the brood and shiver their flight muscles without moving their wings, producing heat. On hot days, the same workers fan their wings at the entrance and spread water droplets inside the hive so that evaporation cools it.',
    q: 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', c: ['Honeybees use different behaviors to keep their hive at a steady temperature.', 'Honeybee colonies are more active on hot days than on cold days.', 'Worker bees use their flight muscles mainly to fly long distances.', 'Water is the most important resource a honeybee colony collects.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The text describes warming behavior (shivering) and cooling behavior (fanning, spreading water), all serving one goal: a steady 35°C. The other choices pick up a word from the text but state ideas it never makes.' },
  { skill: 'cid', d: 1, p: 'When the architect Lina Bo Bardi designed the SESC Pompéia leisure center in São Paulo in the late 1970s, she chose not to demolish the old factory on the site. Instead, she kept its brick sheds and turned them into spaces for reading, theater, and workshops, adding new concrete towers only for the sports facilities that the sheds could not hold.',
    q: 'According to the text, why did Bo Bardi add new concrete towers?', c: ['To house sports facilities that the old sheds could not accommodate', 'To replace brick sheds that were too damaged to reuse', 'To give the factory a more modern appearance', 'To provide reading rooms and theaters for visitors'], a: 0,
    x: 'The last sentence says the towers were added "only for the sports facilities that the sheds could not hold." The sheds were kept, not replaced, and reading and theater went into the sheds.' },
  { skill: 'cid', d: 2, p: 'For decades, researchers assumed that the deep ocean floor received almost no food apart from a slow "snow" of dead plankton. Recent seafloor cameras, however, have recorded whole whale carcasses sinking to the bottom, where they feed dense communities of hagfish, crabs, and worms for years. A single carcass can deliver as much food to a patch of seafloor as two thousand years of marine snow.',
    q: 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', c: ['Sinking whale carcasses are a larger source of food for some deep-sea communities than researchers once assumed.', 'Marine snow is no longer an important source of food for deep-sea animals.', 'Seafloor cameras have replaced older methods of studying the deep ocean.', 'Hagfish, crabs, and worms are the most common animals on the deep ocean floor.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The text sets up an old assumption (almost no food besides marine snow) and corrects it with evidence about whale carcasses. Saying marine snow is "no longer important" goes too far; the text only says carcasses add much more in certain spots.' },
  { skill: 'cid', d: 2, p: 'The following text is from a short story. Marta, a young farm woman, is speaking with her cousin, who lives in town.<br><br>"I like to be where I can see the sun come up," she said, setting down her basket. "In town the houses are so close together that a person never knows what the weather is doing until it is already upon her. Out here I can watch a storm coming across the whole prairie, and I have time to get the chickens in."',
    q: 'Based on the text, what is Marta\'s main reason for preferring the countryside?', c: ['It lets her see what is coming and prepare for it.', 'It is quieter than the crowded town.', 'It allows her to earn more money from her chickens.', 'It reminds her of the place where she was born.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Both of her examples — seeing the sunrise, watching a storm approach "and I have time to get the chickens in" — are about seeing ahead. Quiet, money and birthplace are never mentioned.' },
  { skill: 'cid', d: 3, p: 'Economists have long observed that when a city adds lanes to a congested highway, traffic soon returns to its former level. The usual explanation is "induced demand": wider roads make driving cheaper in time, so people take trips they previously avoided. A 2011 study of U.S. cities by Gilles Duranton and Matthew Turner added a twist. They found that vehicle-miles traveled rose almost exactly in proportion to new road capacity, and that a sizable share of the new traffic came not from residents taking extra trips but from commercial vehicles and from people moving to the area.',
    q: 'Which choice best describes the "twist" that Duranton and Turner added to the usual explanation?', c: ['They showed that part of the extra traffic on new roads came from sources other than existing residents changing their habits.', 'They proved that adding lanes to highways reduces congestion in the long run.', 'They found that commercial traffic declines when highways become congested.', 'They argued that induced demand does not occur in U.S. cities.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The usual explanation focuses on people taking more trips. The study kept the overall finding (traffic rises with capacity) but showed a sizable share of the new traffic came from businesses and newcomers. It did not reject induced demand or show reduced congestion.' },
  { skill: 'cid', d: 3, p: 'In her study of nineteenth-century letters, historian Mai Nguyen noticed that merchants in Hội An often wrote to trading partners about the weather in far more detail than seemed necessary. Rather than treating these passages as polite filler, Nguyen argues that they functioned as coded market reports: a mention of "heavy rains in the hills" told a partner that the coming harvest of cinnamon would be small and that prices would rise.',
    q: 'Which choice best states Nguyen\'s claim about the weather descriptions in the letters?', c: ['They conveyed useful commercial information to readers who knew how to interpret them.', 'They were included mainly to show politeness toward trading partners.', 'They are the most reliable record of nineteenth-century weather in the region.', 'They were written to confuse rival merchants about the price of cinnamon.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Nguyen rejects the "polite filler" reading and says the passages worked as market reports. Nothing says the goal was to confuse rivals, and the letters\' value as weather records is not her point.' },

  /* ── Command of Evidence: Textual ── */
  { skill: 'coe-t', d: 1, p: 'A student is studying whether listening to music while studying affects memory. She claims that the effect depends on whether the music has lyrics.',
    q: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the student\'s claim?', c: ['Participants who studied with instrumental music recalled about as many words as those who studied in silence, while those who studied with songs with lyrics recalled fewer.', 'Participants who studied with music reported enjoying the study session more than those who studied in silence.', 'Participants recalled more words in the morning than in the evening, regardless of music.', 'Participants who studied with songs with lyrics recalled fewer words than those who studied in silence.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The claim compares music WITH lyrics against music WITHOUT lyrics. Only the first finding shows the two kinds of music producing different results. The last choice involves lyrics but never compares them with instrumental music.' },
  { skill: 'coe-t', d: 2, p: '<i>Ode to the West Wind</i> is an 1820 poem by Percy Bysshe Shelley. In the poem, the speaker asks the wind to carry his ideas across the world in the way it carries seeds and leaves.',
    q: 'Which quotation from the poem most effectively illustrates the claim?', c: ['"Drive my dead thoughts over the universe / Like withered leaves to quicken a new birth!"', '"O wild West Wind, thou breath of Autumn\'s being"', '"Thou on whose stream, mid the steep sky\'s commotion, / Loose clouds like earth\'s decaying leaves are shed"', '"If I were a dead leaf thou mightest bear"'], a: 0,
    x: 'The claim has two parts: the speaker asks the wind to spread his IDEAS, and compares this to the way it carries leaves. Only "Drive my dead thoughts… / Like withered leaves" has both his thoughts and the comparison. The others describe the wind or leaves without his ideas.' },
  { skill: 'coe-t', d: 2, p: 'Ecologist Ana Morales hypothesized that urban red foxes are bolder than rural foxes because city foxes are regularly exposed to people without being harmed. She placed an unfamiliar object near feeding sites in both settings and recorded how long foxes took to approach it.',
    q: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support Morales\'s hypothesis?', c: ['Urban foxes approached the unfamiliar object much sooner than rural foxes did.', 'Rural foxes visited feeding sites more often at night than urban foxes did.', 'Both urban and rural foxes eventually ate food placed near the object.', 'Urban foxes had a more varied diet than rural foxes did.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Boldness is measured here by how quickly foxes approach the unfamiliar object. Faster approach by urban foxes is direct support. The other findings are about timing, diet, or show no difference.' },
  { skill: 'coe-t', d: 3, p: 'Some linguists argue that people who speak languages with many words for specific shades of blue — such as Russian, which distinguishes <i>goluboy</i> (light blue) from <i>siniy</i> (dark blue) — are faster at telling those shades apart. Critics respond that any speed difference could come from other cultural factors rather than from the language itself.',
    q: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the critics\' response?', c: ['Russian speakers lost their speed advantage when asked to repeat a string of numbers aloud during the task, a step that interferes with verbal processing.', 'Russian speakers were faster than English speakers at distinguishing shades of blue.', 'English speakers were as fast as Russian speakers at distinguishing shades of green.', 'Russian speakers reported using the words goluboy and siniy many times a day.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The critics say the advantage might come from culture, not language. If blocking verbal processing (and nothing else) erases the advantage, the advantage depends on language — which weakens the critics. Simply showing Russian speakers are faster does not rule out culture.' },
  { skill: 'coe-t', d: 3, p: 'A team of agricultural scientists claims that planting strips of wildflowers along wheat fields reduces crop damage from aphids because the flowers attract hoverflies, whose larvae eat aphids.',
    q: 'Which finding, if true, would most strongly support the team\'s claim?', c: ['Fields with wildflower strips had more hoverfly larvae and fewer aphids than fields without them, and fields where hoverflies were excluded with netting showed no aphid reduction despite the flowers.', 'Fields with wildflower strips had more species of bees than fields without them.', 'Hoverflies were observed feeding on the nectar of several wildflower species.', 'Aphid damage was greater in years with warmer springs.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The claim is a chain: flowers → hoverflies → fewer aphids. The first finding supports every link, and the netting result shows the reduction depends on the hoverflies. The other findings support at most one link.' },
  { skill: 'coe-t', d: 1, p: 'In a survey of 400 public libraries, researchers found that libraries offering evening hours had more teenage visitors. The researchers concluded that teenagers are more likely to visit libraries that are open when school is over.',
    q: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers\' conclusion?', c: ['After several libraries added evening hours, their number of teenage visitors increased.', 'Most adult visitors came to libraries in the morning.', 'Libraries with larger buildings had more visitors of all ages.', 'Teenagers who visited libraries often borrowed more books than adults did.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Seeing teen visits rise after the same libraries added evening hours links the hours to the visits. The other choices are about adults, building size, or borrowing.' },

  /* ── Command of Evidence: Quantitative ── */
  { skill: 'coe-q', d: 1, p: '<table class="data"><caption>Average daily screen time of students (hours)</caption><tr><th>Grade</th><th>2019</th><th>2023</th></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>4.1</td><td>5.6</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>4.8</td><td>6.0</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>5.2</td><td>5.9</td></tr></table>A school counselor reviewed the table and noted that average screen time increased in every grade between 2019 and 2023, with the largest increase among ______',
    q: 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?', c: ['eighth graders.', 'tenth graders.', 'twelfth graders.', 'students in 2019.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Increases: grade 8 rose 1.5 hours (4.1 → 5.6), grade 10 rose 1.2, grade 12 rose 0.7. The largest increase is grade 8, even though grade 10 has the highest 2023 value.' },
  { skill: 'coe-q', d: 2, p: '<table class="data"><caption>Seed germination after 14 days</caption><tr><th>Soil treatment</th><th>Seeds planted</th><th>Seeds germinated</th></tr><tr><td>Untreated</td><td>200</td><td>96</td></tr><tr><td>Compost</td><td>200</td><td>148</td></tr><tr><td>Biochar</td><td>200</td><td>122</td></tr><tr><td>Compost + biochar</td><td>200</td><td>151</td></tr></table>A botanist hypothesized that adding biochar to soil that already contains compost provides little additional benefit to germination.',
    q: 'Which choice best describes data from the table that support the botanist\'s hypothesis?', c: ['The germination count for compost + biochar was only slightly higher than the count for compost alone.', 'More seeds germinated with biochar alone than in untreated soil.', 'Compost alone produced more germinated seeds than biochar alone.', 'Fewer than half of the seeds germinated in untreated soil.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The hypothesis compares compost+biochar with compost alone: 151 vs. 148, a tiny gain. The other choices are accurate but compare the wrong treatments.' },
  { skill: 'coe-q', d: 2, p: '<table class="data"><caption>Share of electricity from wind, Country Z</caption><tr><th>Year</th><th>Share (%)</th></tr><tr><td>2015</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>2017</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>21</td></tr></table>An energy analyst claims that the growth of wind power in Country Z accelerated over the period shown.',
    q: 'Which choice best describes data from the table that support the analyst\'s claim?', c: ['The share rose by 3 percentage points from 2015 to 2017 but by 7 percentage points from 2019 to 2021.', 'The share of electricity from wind was higher in 2021 than in 2015.', 'The share of electricity from wind more than tripled between 2015 and 2021.', 'Wind produced 14 percent of the country\'s electricity in 2019.'], a: 0,
    x: '"Accelerated" means the growth itself got faster. Only comparing the size of the increases (3 points, then 7 points) shows that. Saying it grew or tripled shows growth, not acceleration.' },
  { skill: 'coe-q', d: 3, p: '<table class="data"><caption>Survey: "Would you use a bike lane on Main Street?"</caption><tr><th>Age group</th><th>Yes (%)</th><th>No (%)</th><th>Unsure (%)</th></tr><tr><td>18–29</td><td>58</td><td>27</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>30–49</td><td>44</td><td>41</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>50–64</td><td>31</td><td>52</td><td>17</td></tr><tr><td>65+</td><td>22</td><td>61</td><td>17</td></tr></table>A city planner argues that support for the bike lane declines with age, but that a substantial share of residents in every group has not made up its mind, so an information campaign could still change the overall result.',
    q: 'Which choice best uses data from the table to support the planner\'s argument?', c: ['The percentage answering "yes" falls steadily from 58% to 22% as age increases, while 15% to 17% of every group answered "unsure."', 'In the 18–29 group, more people answered "yes" than "no" or "unsure."', 'The 65+ group had the highest percentage of "no" answers, at 61%.', 'In two of the four age groups, a majority answered "no."'], a: 0,
    x: 'The argument has two parts: support declines with age AND every group has a meaningful undecided share. Only the first choice addresses both.' },
  { skill: 'coe-q', d: 1, p: '<table class="data"><caption>Average length of three bird species (cm)</caption><tr><th>Species</th><th>Length</th></tr><tr><td>Blue tit</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>European robin</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>Common starling</td><td>21</td></tr></table>Of the three species shown, the ______',
    q: 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?', c: ['common starling is the longest, at about 21 cm.', 'blue tit is the longest, at about 12 cm.', 'European robin is the shortest, at about 14 cm.', 'common starling is the shortest, at about 21 cm.'], a: 0,
    x: '21 cm is the greatest length in the table, and it belongs to the starling. The other choices misread which value is largest or smallest.' },

  /* ── Inferences ── */
  { skill: 'inf', d: 1, p: 'Most cacti store water in their thick stems and have spines instead of broad leaves, which reduces water loss. The Christmas cactus, however, is native to humid mountain forests in Brazil, where it grows on tree branches in shade and frequent mist. Gardeners who treat it like a desert cactus, giving it full sun and very little water, therefore often find that ______',
    q: 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c: ['the plant does poorly under conditions that suit other cacti.', 'the plant grows larger than it would in a forest.', 'the plant develops more spines to protect itself.', 'desert cacti are easier to grow indoors than outdoors.'], a: 0,
    x: '"However" signals that this cactus is different: it comes from humid, shady forests. Treating it like a desert cactus therefore gives it the wrong conditions, so it does poorly.' },
  { skill: 'inf', d: 2, p: 'Ancient Roman concrete has survived for two thousand years in seawater, while modern concrete in similar conditions often cracks within decades. Researchers found that Roman builders mixed volcanic ash with lime and seawater, and that seawater seeping into the old concrete slowly grows new mineral crystals in its cracks. This suggests that the Roman material ______',
    q: 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c: ['becomes stronger over time through contact with the very conditions that damage modern concrete.', 'was used only in buildings that were never exposed to water.', 'was cheaper to produce than modern concrete.', 'cannot be reproduced because volcanic ash no longer exists.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Seawater, which damages modern concrete, grows new crystals in Roman concrete\'s cracks — so the same conditions strengthen it. Cost and the availability of ash are never discussed.' },
  { skill: 'inf', d: 2, p: 'In a classic experiment, children were offered one marshmallow now or two if they waited fifteen minutes. Children who waited were later found to have higher test scores. A 2018 replication with a larger, more diverse sample found that the link mostly disappeared once researchers accounted for family income and home environment. Taken together, these findings suggest that ______',
    q: 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c: ['the ability to wait may reflect children\'s circumstances more than it independently predicts later achievement.', 'children from all backgrounds are equally able to wait fifteen minutes for a reward.', 'the original experiment did not actually measure whether children waited.', 'test scores are not affected by family income or home environment.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Once income and home environment were accounted for, the link vanished, meaning those background factors likely explained it. The last choice contradicts the passage.' },
  { skill: 'inf', d: 3, p: 'Archaeologists excavating a 9,000-year-old village in Anatolia found that the houses had no doors at ground level; residents entered through openings in the roofs. The houses were built wall to wall with no streets between them, and the roofs appear to have served as walkways. Because entering any house meant walking across the roofs of neighboring houses, archaeologists infer that residents ______',
    q: 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c: ['routinely moved through spaces above their neighbors\' homes as part of daily life.', 'rarely left their homes once they had entered them.', 'built roof openings mainly to let smoke escape.', 'were in constant conflict with neighboring villages.'], a: 0,
    x: 'With no streets and roof-level entrances, getting anywhere meant crossing other roofs. The inference stays close to that layout; smoke and conflict are not supported.' },
  { skill: 'inf', d: 3, p: 'Many flowering plants depend on a single type of pollinator. When biologist Jun Park studied a mountain orchid, she found it was visited almost exclusively by one species of moth. Over twenty years, warming temperatures caused the orchid to bloom about nine days earlier, while the moth\'s emergence date did not change. If this pattern continues, the orchid will most likely ______',
    q: 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c: ['produce fewer seeds, since many flowers will open before its pollinator is active.', 'attract a wider range of pollinators to replace the moth.', 'begin blooming later to match the moth.', 'spread to lower, warmer elevations.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The orchid relies on one moth, and its bloom is moving earlier while the moth is not. A growing mismatch means fewer pollinated flowers — fewer seeds. The other outcomes are not supported by the text.' },
  { skill: 'inf', d: 1, p: 'The town library began lending tools such as drills and ladders in 2021. In the first year, tool loans were highest in April and May and lowest in December and January. Library staff concluded that demand for tools is likely ______',
    q: 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c: ['higher in spring, when many people start home and garden projects.', 'the same in every month of the year.', 'higher among children than among adults.', 'lower than demand for books in every month.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Loans peaked in April and May (spring) and dropped in winter. Nothing in the text concerns age or books.' },

  /* ── Words in Context ── */
  { skill: 'wic', d: 1, p: 'Although the first reviews of the novel were harsh, readers ______ it: within a year it had sold more copies than any other book that season.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', c: ['embraced', 'ignored', 'criticized', 'misplaced'], a: 0,
    x: '"Although" contrasts the harsh reviews with readers\' reaction, and the sales figure shows enthusiasm. "Embraced" fits; "ignored" and "criticized" contradict the sales.' },
  { skill: 'wic', d: 1, p: 'The bridge was designed to be ______: its parts were made in a factory and could be assembled at the site in only a few days.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', c: ['modular', 'ornate', 'fragile', 'ancient'], a: 0,
    x: 'The colon introduces an explanation: prefabricated parts assembled quickly. That describes a modular design.' },
  { skill: 'wic', d: 2, p: 'Biologist Tomás Ruiz is known for being ______ in his fieldwork: he records the time, temperature, and location of every observation, even ones he expects to discard later.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', c: ['meticulous', 'impulsive', 'reluctant', 'optimistic'], a: 0,
    x: 'Recording every detail of every observation is the definition of meticulous (extremely careful). Nothing suggests he is impulsive, reluctant or optimistic.' },
  { skill: 'wic', d: 2, p: 'The committee\'s report did not reject the proposal outright, but its long list of unresolved concerns made its ______ attitude clear to anyone who read it.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', c: ['skeptical', 'enthusiastic', 'indifferent', 'hostile'], a: 0,
    x: 'Not rejecting outright, yet listing many concerns, suggests doubt — skeptical. "Hostile" is too strong because it did not reject the plan; "indifferent" ignores the long list of concerns.' },
  { skill: 'wic', d: 3, p: 'The historian argues that the treaty\'s importance has been ______ by later writers: although they describe it as a turning point, it changed little in practice, and trade between the two nations continued exactly as before.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', c: ['overstated', 'documented', 'questioned', 'concealed'], a: 0,
    x: 'Later writers call it a turning point, but it changed little — so they have made it seem more important than it was: overstated. "Questioned" would mean the writers doubted it, the opposite of what they do.' },
  { skill: 'wic', d: 3, p: 'Early critics dismissed the composer\'s use of street songs and folk rhythms as a gimmick, but later scholars saw it as ______ choice, part of a deliberate effort to bring everyday sounds into the concert hall.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', c: ['a principled', 'an accidental', 'a fashionable', 'a careless'], a: 0,
    x: 'Later scholars disagree with "gimmick" and call it part of a "deliberate effort," so the choice was principled. "Accidental" and "careless" contradict "deliberate."' },
  { skill: 'wic', d: 2, p: 'In the dry season the river slows to a trickle, but heavy rains can ______ it within hours, turning the shallow stream into a fast-moving torrent.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', c: ['swell', 'drain', 'calm', 'divert'], a: 0,
    x: 'The rains turn a trickle into a torrent — they make it bigger: swell. "Drain" and "calm" go the wrong way.' },

  /* ── Text Structure and Purpose ── */
  { skill: 'tsp', d: 1, p: 'Many people assume that bats are blind. <u>In fact, most bat species can see quite well, and some fruit bats have vision sharper than that of humans in dim light.</u> Bats\' reliance on echolocation for hunting insects in darkness may explain how the myth arose.',
    q: 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?', c: ['It corrects a common belief introduced in the previous sentence.', 'It offers an example that supports the common belief.', 'It explains how bats use echolocation.', 'It introduces a question that the text leaves unanswered.'], a: 0,
    x: '"In fact" signals a correction: the first sentence states the belief, the underlined sentence shows it is wrong.' },
  { skill: 'tsp', d: 2, p: 'The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, dug into a mountain in Arctic Norway, holds more than a million seed samples from around the world. The site was chosen because the permafrost keeps the seeds cold even if the power fails, and its height above sea level protects it from flooding. In 2015, researchers withdrew seeds stored there to rebuild a collection in Syria that had been lost during the war.',
    q: 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', c: ['It describes a facility, explains why its location was chosen, and gives an example of its use.', 'It presents a problem with a facility and proposes a solution.', 'It compares two seed collections and argues that one is better.', 'It traces the history of seed storage from ancient times to the present.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Sentence 1 describes the vault, sentence 2 explains the location, sentence 3 gives the Syria example. No problem is solved and no comparison is made.' },
  { skill: 'tsp', d: 2, p: 'The following text is from a 1905 short story.<br><br>Mr. Hale had been a clerk at the bank for thirty years and had never once arrived late. Each morning he hung his coat on the third hook, set his pen to the right of the ledger, and opened the window exactly two inches. <u>On the morning the new manager arrived, the window was closed, and the third hook was empty.</u>',
    q: 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?', c: ['It signals that something has disrupted the routine that the previous sentences establish.', 'It describes the new manager\'s appearance.', 'It explains why Mr. Hale had never been late.', 'It reveals that Mr. Hale dislikes fresh air.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The earlier sentences build a precise routine; the underlined sentence shows that routine broken (closed window, empty hook), hinting something has changed.' },
  { skill: 'tsp', d: 3, p: 'Researchers have long debated whether early farming spread through Europe because farmers migrated or because hunter-gatherers adopted farming from their neighbors. Ancient DNA has helped resolve the question in some regions: in central Europe, the genes of early farmers closely match those of populations in Anatolia, indicating migration. <u>In the Baltic region, however, hunter-gatherer ancestry remained dominant long after farming began.</u>',
    q: 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?', c: ['It indicates that the answer to the debate may differ from one region to another.', 'It shows that ancient DNA cannot be used to study the spread of farming.', 'It provides further evidence that farming spread mainly through migration.', 'It explains why farming began earlier in central Europe than in the Baltic.'], a: 0,
    x: '"In some regions" and "however" prepare a contrast: central Europe shows migration, but the Baltic shows locals adopting farming. So the answer varies by region. It does not dismiss DNA evidence.' },
  { skill: 'tsp', d: 3, p: 'Some economists claim that raising the minimum wage necessarily reduces employment. A 1994 study of fast-food restaurants in New Jersey and neighboring Pennsylvania found no such drop after New Jersey raised its wage. Later studies have reached mixed conclusions, and many economists now argue that the effect depends on how large the increase is and on local labor conditions.',
    q: 'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', c: ['To show how a confident claim has been complicated by evidence', 'To argue that the minimum wage should be raised in every state', 'To explain why fast-food restaurants pay low wages', 'To criticize the methods used in a 1994 study'], a: 0,
    x: 'The text starts with a strong claim ("necessarily"), presents contrary evidence, and ends with a more nuanced view. It does not argue for policy or attack the study.' },

  /* ── Cross-Text Connections ── */
  { skill: 'ctc', d: 2, p: '<b>Text 1</b><br>Many urban planners argue that cities should replace parking lots with apartments. More housing near jobs and transit, they say, would lower rents and cut commuting time.<br><br><b>Text 2</b><br>Economist Rita Osei agrees that building homes near transit can lower rents, but she found that in cities with poor bus and train service, residents of new apartments still drove to work. Removing parking without first improving transit, she argues, may simply move traffic onto nearby streets.',
    q: 'Based on the texts, how would Osei (Text 2) most likely respond to the planners\' argument in Text 1?', c: ['She would agree that the plan can lower rents but caution that its benefits for commuting depend on good transit.', 'She would reject the plan because new housing never lowers rents.', 'She would support the plan without reservation.', 'She would argue that cities should build more parking lots instead of apartments.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Osei "agrees" about rents but adds a condition: without good transit, commuting will not improve. That is partial agreement, not rejection.' },
  { skill: 'ctc', d: 3, p: '<b>Text 1</b><br>The extinction of large mammals such as mammoths at the end of the last ice age has often been blamed on human hunters. Sites in North America show that many species vanished soon after people arrived, a timing that seems hard to explain otherwise.<br><br><b>Text 2</b><br>Paleoecologist Kim Lee notes that the same period saw rapid climate change that shrank the grasslands these animals depended on. She points out that in regions where humans arrived thousands of years before the climate shifted, large mammals survived until the shift occurred.',
    q: 'Based on the texts, how would Lee (Text 2) most likely respond to the reasoning in Text 1?', c: ['She would argue that the timing cited in Text 1 does not rule out climate change as the main cause.', 'She would agree that human hunting alone explains the extinctions.', 'She would argue that mammoths did not depend on grasslands.', 'She would claim that humans never hunted large mammals.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Text 1 rests on timing ("hard to explain otherwise"). Lee offers another explanation for the same period, and evidence that humans alone did not cause extinction. So she would challenge the reasoning, not deny hunting occurred.' },
  { skill: 'ctc', d: 2, p: '<b>Text 1</b><br>Handwriting notes is slower than typing, which some teachers see as a drawback. Students who type can capture nearly everything a lecturer says.<br><br><b>Text 2</b><br>In experiments by Pam Mueller and Daniel Oppenheimer, students who typed notes wrote down more words but often copied lectures word for word. Students who wrote by hand had to summarize as they went, and they performed better on conceptual questions afterward.',
    q: 'Based on the texts, how would Mueller and Oppenheimer (Text 2) most likely respond to the view of the teachers in Text 1?', c: ['The slowness of handwriting may be an advantage because it forces students to process ideas.', 'Typing is better because students capture more of the lecture.', 'Students should not take notes during lectures at all.', 'Handwriting and typing lead to identical learning.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Text 1 calls slowness a drawback. Text 2 shows that the need to summarize — caused by slowness — improved understanding, turning the supposed drawback into a benefit.' },

  /* ── Rhetorical Synthesis ── */
  { skill: 'rs', d: 1, p: 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<ul><li>The Great Wall of China is a series of fortifications built over many centuries.</li><li>Its total length, including all branches, is about 21,000 km.</li><li>The best-known sections were built during the Ming dynasty (1368–1644).</li></ul>',
    q: 'The student wants to emphasize how long the wall is. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c: ['Including all of its branches, the Great Wall of China stretches about 21,000 km.', 'The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries.', 'Many of the best-known sections of the Great Wall were built during the Ming dynasty.', 'The Great Wall of China is a series of fortifications.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The goal is length, and only the first choice gives the 21,000 km figure.' },
  { skill: 'rs', d: 2, p: 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<ul><li>Octavia Butler (1947–2006) was an American science fiction writer.</li><li><i>Kindred</i> (1979) follows a modern woman pulled back in time to a plantation in the 1800s.</li><li><i>Parable of the Sower</i> (1993) is set in a future California damaged by climate change.</li><li>Butler was the first science fiction writer to receive a MacArthur Fellowship.</li></ul>',
    q: 'The student wants to contrast the settings of two of Butler\'s novels. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c: ['While Butler\'s <i>Kindred</i> sends its main character into the past, her <i>Parable of the Sower</i> takes place in a future California.', 'Octavia Butler wrote both <i>Kindred</i> and <i>Parable of the Sower</i>.', 'Butler, an American science fiction writer, was the first in her genre to receive a MacArthur Fellowship.', '<i>Kindred</i>, published in 1979, follows a modern woman who travels to the 1800s.'], a: 0,
    x: 'A contrast of settings needs both novels and their settings. Only the first choice includes both and uses "While" to contrast them.' },
  { skill: 'rs', d: 3, p: 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<ul><li>Tardigrades are microscopic animals about 0.5 mm long.</li><li>They can survive near-total dehydration by entering a state called cryptobiosis.</li><li>In 2007, tardigrades were exposed to the vacuum of space for ten days aboard a satellite.</li><li>Many of them survived and later reproduced normally.</li></ul>',
    q: 'The student wants to present the 2007 experiment to an audience unfamiliar with tardigrades. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c: ['In 2007, tardigrades — microscopic animals that can survive extreme dehydration — spent ten days in the vacuum of space, and many survived.', 'In 2007, many tardigrades survived ten days in the vacuum of space aboard a satellite.', 'Tardigrades, which are about 0.5 mm long, can enter a state called cryptobiosis.', 'After ten days in space, many tardigrades later reproduced normally.'], a: 0,
    x: 'An unfamiliar audience needs to know what a tardigrade is, and the goal requires the experiment. The first choice does both; the second describes the experiment without explaining tardigrades.' },
  { skill: 'rs', d: 2, p: 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<ul><li>Study A followed 1,200 adults who walked at least 7,000 steps a day.</li><li>They had a 50% lower risk of early death than those who walked under 7,000 steps.</li><li>Study B found that walking pace mattered less than the total number of steps.</li></ul>',
    q: 'The student wants to emphasize a finding about walking speed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c: ['According to Study B, how fast people walk matters less than how many steps they take in total.', 'Study A followed 1,200 adults who walked at least 7,000 steps a day.', 'Adults who walked at least 7,000 steps a day had a 50% lower risk of early death.', 'Two studies examined the health effects of walking.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Only Study B\'s finding is about pace (speed).' },

  /* ── Transitions ── */
  { skill: 'tr', d: 1, p: 'Venus is the planet closest in size to Earth. ______ its surface is far hotter than Earth\'s, averaging about 465°C because its thick atmosphere traps heat.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', c: ['However,', 'For example,', 'Therefore,', 'Similarly,'], a: 0,
    x: 'Similar size, very different temperature: a contrast. "However" signals contrast; "Therefore" would mean the heat results from its size.' },
  { skill: 'tr', d: 1, p: 'The city installed sensors that adjust traffic lights based on the number of waiting cars. ______ the average wait at major intersections fell by nearly a third.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', c: ['As a result,', 'Nevertheless,', 'For instance,', 'In contrast,'], a: 0,
    x: 'The shorter wait is an effect of the sensors — cause → effect.' },
  { skill: 'tr', d: 2, p: 'Many desert animals avoid the heat by being active only at night. Others have physical adaptations. ______ the fennec fox\'s enormous ears release body heat, helping it stay cool.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', c: ['For example,', 'Still,', 'Consequently,', 'In other words,'], a: 0,
    x: 'The fennec fox\'s ears illustrate the "physical adaptations" just mentioned, so the sentence is an example.' },
  { skill: 'tr', d: 2, p: 'The museum\'s new exhibit drew record crowds in its first month. ______ it received mixed reviews from critics, several of whom found its layout confusing.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', c: ['Even so,', 'Thus,', 'Additionally,', 'Specifically,'], a: 0,
    x: 'Record crowds but mixed reviews: a contrast. "Even so" concedes the first point and introduces a contrasting one. "Additionally" would treat the mixed reviews as another success.' },
  { skill: 'tr', d: 3, p: 'Early maps of the Pacific often placed islands in the wrong locations because sailors had no reliable way to measure longitude at sea. Errors of a hundred miles were common. ______ some islands were "discovered" several times by explorers who did not realize they had found a place already on the map.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', c: ['Consequently,', 'Nonetheless,', 'By contrast,', 'Previously,'], a: 0,
    x: 'Because islands were mislocated, explorers rediscovered them — an effect of the errors.' },
  { skill: 'tr', d: 3, p: 'Composer Florence Price was the first Black woman to have a symphony performed by a major American orchestra, in 1933. For decades after her death, ______ much of her music went unplayed; dozens of her manuscripts were found in an abandoned house only in 2009.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', c: ['however,', 'moreover,', 'for example,', 'similarly,'], a: 0,
    x: 'Her early success contrasts with later neglect.' },
  { skill: 'tr', d: 2, p: 'The first step in the recipe is to soak the beans overnight. ______ the beans are drained and simmered for about an hour.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', c: ['Then', 'However,', 'In conclusion,', 'For instance,'], a: 0,
    x: 'The sentence describes the next step in a sequence.' },

  /* ── Boundaries ── */
  { skill: 'bnd', d: 1, p: 'The marathon route passes through all five boroughs of New York ______ runners cross five bridges along the way.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['City; in fact,', 'City, in fact,', 'City in fact,', 'City, in fact'], a: 0,
    x: 'Both sides are complete sentences, so they need a semicolon (or period). A comma alone creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.' },
  { skill: 'bnd', d: 1, p: 'Glaciers move extremely ______ some advance only a few centimeters each day.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['slowly:', 'slowly,', 'slowly', 'slowly, and,'], a: 0,
    x: 'The second clause explains the first, and both are complete sentences. A colon works; a comma alone would be a splice.' },
  { skill: 'bnd', d: 2, p: 'The poet Joy Harjo, who served three terms as U.S. Poet ______ often incorporates the rhythms of jazz into her writing.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['Laureate,', 'Laureate', 'Laureate;', 'Laureate—'], a: 0,
    x: 'The clause "who served three terms…" is extra information opened by a comma, so it must close with a comma. A dash cannot close a phrase opened with a comma.' },
  { skill: 'bnd', d: 2, p: 'The researchers collected three kinds of ______ soil cores, water samples, and air-quality readings.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['data:', 'data;', 'data,', 'data, such as:'], a: 0,
    x: 'A complete sentence followed by a list: use a colon. A semicolon needs a complete sentence after it; "such as:" puts a colon after an incomplete phrase.' },
  { skill: 'bnd', d: 3, p: 'Among the most surprising findings of the ______ that some fungi can transfer nutrients between trees through underground networks.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['study was', 'study, was', 'study—was', 'study; was'], a: 0,
    x: 'The subject ("Among the most surprising findings of the study") runs straight into the verb "was." Nothing should separate a subject from its verb.' },
  { skill: 'bnd', d: 3, p: 'Physicist Chien-Shiung Wu designed the 1956 experiment that overturned the law of parity ______ the two colleagues who had proposed the idea received the Nobel Prize, but she did not.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['conservation; however,', 'conservation, however,', 'conservation however', 'conservation, however;'], a: 0,
    x: '"However" begins a new complete sentence, so the break goes before it (semicolon) and a comma follows it.' },
  { skill: 'bnd', d: 2, p: 'In 1977 the Voyager probes were launched with a "Golden Record" ______ greetings in 55 languages, music, and natural sounds from Earth.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['containing', 'containing:', 'containing,', 'containing;'], a: 0,
    x: 'The list is the direct object of "containing," so no punctuation goes between the verb and its object.' },

  /* ── Form, Structure, and Sense ── */
  { skill: 'fss', d: 1, p: 'The collection of rare maps, which includes charts drawn by sixteenth-century navigators, ______ displayed in the library\'s east wing.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['is', 'are', 'have been', 'were being'], a: 0,
    x: 'The subject is "collection" (singular), not "maps" or "charts." Singular subject → "is."' },
  { skill: 'fss', d: 1, p: 'Each of the volunteers at the shelter ______ a training course before working with the animals.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['completes', 'complete', 'are completing', 'have completed'], a: 0,
    x: '"Each" is singular, so it takes "completes."' },
  { skill: 'fss', d: 2, p: 'When the city council approved the new budget, ______ set aside funds for repairing three historic bridges.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['it', 'they', 'those', 'he or she'], a: 0,
    x: '"The city council" is a single body, so the pronoun is "it."' },
  { skill: 'fss', d: 2, p: 'Built from local limestone in the 1880s, ______',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['the courthouse still dominates the town square.', 'visitors still admire the courthouse in the town square.', 'the town square is still dominated by the courthouse.', 'many residents consider the courthouse a landmark.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The opening phrase describes something built from limestone, so the courthouse must come right after the comma. Otherwise visitors, the square, or residents would be "built from limestone."' },
  { skill: 'fss', d: 2, p: 'By the time the rescue team reached the summit, the storm ______ for nearly six hours.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['had been raging', 'rages', 'will rage', 'is raging'], a: 0,
    x: 'An action continuing up to a point in the past ("by the time… reached") calls for the past perfect progressive: "had been raging."' },
  { skill: 'fss', d: 3, p: 'The novelist\'s early drafts, now held in a university archive, reveal how carefully she revised ______ characters\' dialogue, sometimes rewriting a single line a dozen times.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['her', 'their', 'its', 'there'], a: 0,
    x: 'The pronoun refers to the novelist, who is "she," so "her." "Their" would refer to the drafts.' },
  { skill: 'fss', d: 3, p: 'Neither the lead engineer nor the members of her team ______ that the prototype would pass every test on its first trial.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['were expecting', 'was expecting', 'expects', 'has expected'], a: 0,
    x: 'With "neither… nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "members" (plural), and the past context needs "were expecting."' },
  { skill: 'fss', d: 1, p: 'The museum reopened after renovations, and ______ new galleries now include an interactive science wing.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['its', 'it\'s', 'its\'', 'their'], a: 0,
    x: 'Possessive of "museum" (singular): "its." "It\'s" means "it is."' },
  { skill: 'fss', d: 2, p: 'The three ______ findings were published together in a single journal issue.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', c: ['researchers\'', 'researcher\'s', 'researchers', 'researchers\'s'], a: 0,
    x: 'Several researchers own the findings: plural possessive "researchers\'."' },
];

/* ── second set (appended, so the ids of the questions above never change) ── */
RW_BANK.push(
  { skill: 'ctc', d: 1, p: '<b>Text 1</b><br>Many coaches believe young athletes should specialize in one sport early, since thousands of hours of focused practice build elite skill.<br><br><b>Text 2</b><br>Sports scientist Lena Ortiz tracked 1,200 young athletes and found that those who played several sports until about age 14 were injured less often and were just as likely to reach the national level as early specialists.',
    q: 'Based on the texts, how would Ortiz (Text 2) most likely respond to the coaches\' belief in Text 1?', c: ['She would argue that early specialization is not necessary to reach a high level and may carry risks.', 'She would agree that early specialization is the best path to elite skill.', 'She would argue that young athletes should not play sports at all.', 'She would claim that practice has no effect on skill.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Ortiz found multi-sport athletes reached the national level just as often, with fewer injuries — so specializing early is not needed and has costs. She never says practice does not matter.' },
  { skill: 'ctc', d: 2, p: '<b>Text 1</b><br>Some historians describe the printing press as the single cause of the rapid spread of literacy in sixteenth-century Europe: cheap books, they argue, created readers.<br><br><b>Text 2</b><br>Historian Ana Reyes points out that in several regions, literacy rates were already rising before printers arrived, driven by town schools and merchants who needed written records. Printing, she argues, met a growing demand as much as it created one.',
    q: 'Based on the texts, how would Reyes (Text 2) most likely describe the view presented in Text 1?', c: ['As overlooking evidence that the demand for reading was already growing for other reasons', 'As correct in claiming that printing alone created new readers', 'As mistaken in suggesting that printing existed in the sixteenth century', 'As exaggerating how many books merchants needed'], a: 0,
    x: 'Text 1 calls printing the "single cause." Reyes shows literacy was rising beforehand for other reasons, so she would say Text 1 overlooks that evidence. She does not deny printing\'s role.' },
  { skill: 'ctc', d: 3, p: '<b>Text 1</b><br>Biologists have long assumed that birdsong is learned mainly by males, since in many well-studied northern species only males sing. Female song, when it occurs, has often been treated as a rare exception.<br><br><b>Text 2</b><br>A 2014 survey of more than 1,000 songbird species by Karan Odom and colleagues found female song in 71 percent of species where data were available, and concluded that the common ancestor of modern songbirds most likely had singing females. The traditional picture, they argue, came from studying a small set of species in Europe and North America.',
    q: 'Based on the texts, how would Odom and colleagues (Text 2) most likely respond to the assumption described in Text 1?', c: ['They would argue that it reflects a narrow sample of species rather than songbirds as a whole.', 'They would agree that female song is a rare exception among songbirds.', 'They would argue that male songbirds do not learn their songs.', 'They would claim that northern species should not be studied.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Text 2 says female song is widespread and that the old view came from studying a limited set of northern species — a sampling problem.' },
  { skill: 'rs', d: 1, p: 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<ul><li>The James Webb Space Telescope launched in December 2021.</li><li>Its main mirror is 6.5 meters across.</li><li>It observes mainly in infrared light.</li><li>Infrared light can pass through dust clouds that block visible light.</li></ul>',
    q: 'The student wants to explain why the telescope uses infrared light. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c: ['The Webb telescope observes in infrared, which can pass through dust clouds that block visible light.', 'The Webb telescope, launched in December 2021, has a 6.5-meter mirror.', 'The Webb telescope\'s main mirror is 6.5 meters across.', 'The Webb telescope launched in 2021 and observes mainly in infrared light.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The goal is the REASON for infrared. Only the first choice gives it: infrared passes through dust that blocks visible light.' },
  { skill: 'rs', d: 2, p: 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<ul><li>Mangrove forests grow along tropical coastlines.</li><li>Their roots trap sediment and slow incoming waves.</li><li>A 2020 study estimated that mangroves prevent more than $65 billion in flood damage each year.</li><li>Many mangrove forests have been cleared for shrimp farms.</li></ul>',
    q: 'The student wants to emphasize the economic value of mangroves. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c: ['A 2020 study estimated that mangroves prevent more than $65 billion in flood damage each year.', 'Mangrove forests grow along tropical coastlines around the world.', 'Mangrove roots trap sediment and slow incoming waves.', 'Many mangrove forests have been cleared for shrimp farms.'], a: 0,
    x: '"Economic value" calls for the dollar figure — $65 billion in prevented damage.' },
  { skill: 'rs', d: 3, p: 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<ul><li>Hedy Lamarr (1914–2000) was a film actress.</li><li>In 1942 she and composer George Antheil patented a "frequency-hopping" system to keep radio-guided torpedoes from being jammed.</li><li>The idea was not used by the U.S. Navy during World War II.</li><li>Frequency hopping later became one of the ideas behind Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.</li></ul>',
    q: 'The student wants to emphasize the long-term significance of Lamarr and Antheil\'s invention. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c: ['Though unused in World War II, the frequency-hopping system Lamarr and Antheil patented in 1942 later helped make Wi-Fi and Bluetooth possible.', 'In 1942, actress Hedy Lamarr and composer George Antheil patented a frequency-hopping system.', 'Lamarr and Antheil designed their system to keep radio-guided torpedoes from being jammed.', 'The U.S. Navy did not use Lamarr and Antheil\'s frequency-hopping system during World War II.'], a: 0,
    x: '"Long-term significance" requires the later impact: Wi-Fi and Bluetooth. Only the first choice includes it.' },
  { skill: 'tsp', d: 1, p: 'Sea otters often float on their backs and use a rock to crack open clams on their chests. Researchers have found rocks along the California coast with distinctive dents and shell fragments around them. <u>By studying these worn "anvil stones," scientists can identify where otters fed even in places where no one saw them.</u>',
    q: 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?', c: ['It explains how evidence described earlier can be used by researchers.', 'It introduces the behavior that the rest of the text describes.', 'It argues that sea otters are disappearing from California.', 'It contrasts sea otters with other tool-using animals.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The earlier sentences describe the dented rocks; the underlined sentence explains what scientists can learn from them.' },
  { skill: 'coe-q', d: 3, p: '<table class="data"><caption>Average commute time (minutes) by mode, City Q</caption><tr><th>Mode</th><th>2015</th><th>2022</th></tr><tr><td>Car</td><td>31</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><td>Bus</td><td>42</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>Bicycle</td><td>24</td><td>22</td></tr></table>After City Q added bus-only lanes in 2018, a transit official claimed that the lanes made bus trips faster while car commutes did not benefit.',
    q: 'Which choice best describes data from the table that support the official\'s claim?', c: ['Average bus commutes fell from 42 to 38 minutes, while average car commutes rose from 31 to 36 minutes.', 'Bicycle commutes were the shortest of the three modes in both years.', 'Average car commutes were shorter than bus commutes in 2015.', 'Average bicycle commutes fell from 24 to 22 minutes.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The claim has two parts: buses got faster, cars did not benefit. The first choice gives both. Bicycle data is irrelevant to the claim.' },
  { skill: 'inf', d: 2, p: 'Light pollution is known to disrupt night-flying insects, which circle streetlights until exhausted. In a recent field study, ecologists replaced white streetlights with red ones along one road and left a similar road unchanged. Moth counts near the red lights soon returned to the levels found in fully dark areas. These results suggest that ______',
    q: 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c: ['the color of artificial light may matter as much as its presence for night-flying insects.', 'moths are not affected by any kind of artificial light.', 'streetlights should be removed from all roads.', 'red light attracts more moths than white light does.'], a: 0,
    x: 'Changing only the color brought moth numbers back to dark-area levels, so color matters. The data do not show that no light affects moths or that lights should be removed.' },
  { skill: 'cid', d: 2, p: 'Many people picture glaciers as frozen and still, but they move constantly. Under their own weight, glaciers slowly deform and slide over meltwater at their base. In Greenland, some outlet glaciers travel more than 40 meters a day, carrying ice from the interior to the sea, where it breaks off as icebergs.',
    q: 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', c: ['Glaciers are always moving, sometimes quickly, even though they may seem still.', 'Greenland has more glaciers than any other place on Earth.', 'Icebergs are dangerous to ships in the North Atlantic.', 'Glaciers melt faster in summer than in winter.'], a: 0,
    x: 'The text corrects the image of still glaciers and shows they move constantly — sometimes 40 meters a day. The other choices introduce ideas the text never discusses.' },
  { skill: 'wic', d: 1, p: 'The new bridge was built to be ______: engineers expect it to last at least 150 years with only routine maintenance.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', c: ['durable', 'temporary', 'decorative', 'affordable'], a: 0,
    x: 'Lasting 150 years with little maintenance means durable. "Temporary" is the opposite; cost and decoration are not mentioned.' },
  { skill: 'tr', d: 1, p: 'Octopuses can change the color of their skin in a fraction of a second. ______ they can change its texture, raising small bumps to look like rock or coral.',
    q: 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', c: ['Moreover,', 'However,', 'As a result,', 'In contrast,'], a: 0,
    x: 'The second sentence adds a second ability to the first: addition.' },
);
